# Copy to .env and fill in. Never commit .env.

# --- Postgres ---
# Neon / Vercel Postgres: use the *pooled* URL for the app.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/DATABASE?schema=public"
# Direct (unpooled) connection — required by `prisma migrate` against a pooled host.
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/DATABASE?schema=public"

# --- The shell's own auth (nothing to do with Base44's) ---
# Auth.js v5 accepts the v4 names. `openssl rand -base64 32` for the secret.
# Every deployment that signs in through the redirect proxy below must hold the SAME
# secret: the OAuth `state` is encrypted with it.
NEXTAUTH_SECRET=""
# The deployment origin. Setting it *overrides* the origin NextAuth reads off the
# request, so scope it to production only — a preview deploy that inherited
# production's value would believe it is production and sign the user in there.
# Previews leave it unset and take their host from the request. See docs/deploy.md.
NEXTAUTH_URL="http://localhost:3000"
# Google OAuth client (Web application). Authorized redirect URI:
#   $NEXTAUTH_URL/api/auth/callback/google
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=""
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=""
# Makes preview deployments signable-in without registering their URLs with Google,
# which is impossible: there is a new URL per pull request and Google allows no
# wildcard. Every deployment sends Google *production's* callback and encodes its own
# origin in the OAuth `state`; production forwards the callback to that origin.
#
# Set it on production and on the preview contexts alike — the deployment whose own
# origin matches this URL is the one that forwards. Note the `/api/auth` suffix, and
# leave it unset locally, where the direct flow works.
#   AUTH_REDIRECT_PROXY_URL="https://<your-production-host>/api/auth"
AUTH_REDIRECT_PROXY_URL=""
# Not set by hand anywhere. On Netlify, a preview pins its own origin to the deploy
# alias by reading Netlify's DEPLOY_PRIME_URL (baked at build time by next.config.ts),
# because the request host a preview sees is the deploy permalink, not the alias its
# users are on. Setting AUTH_URL yourself overrides that and takes precedence over
# NEXTAUTH_URL. See docs/deploy.md → Preview deployments.
#   AUTH_URL=""

# --- Base44 app-factory bridge, server-only ---
# Without these the app runs fine; only the builder is unavailable, and it shows
# its "connect" state instead (the bridge answers 501 bridge_misconfigured).
#
# The `b44k_` workspace API key used on the hot path: minting, and signing a
# viewer into an embedded app. Needs `user_tokens:mint`, plus
# `app_users:provision` + `app_users:embed_token` for embedding — those two are
# `requires_app_targeting` upstream, so the key's app access must be **all
# apps**, or each new app would need adding before its first frame could sign in. Sent in Authorization *bare*, no "Bearer". The most
# sensitive value here — it can vend a token acting as any service principal in
# the workspace.
BASE44_SVC_KEY=""
# Optional second key for the two privileged calls, provision + deprovision
# (`service_users:provision`). Defaults to BASE44_SVC_KEY when unset, which is
# fine to start with. Splitting them is the stronger posture: a mint-only key on
# the hot path is what makes deprovisioning actually stick, because a
# provision-capable key lets a removed user press Connect and come straight back.
BASE44_PROVISION_KEY=""
# Not secrets — the workspace and folder ids, and the platform host. In env
# because the host rotates and the ids become per-tenant later. Never
# caller-supplied: a request-controlled host here would be an SSRF, and a
# request-controlled workspace/folder id would defeat the tenancy boundary.
BASE44_ORG_ID=""
# NB a Base44 PR-preview host, and it rotates — re-resolve if calls start 404ing.
BASE44_PLATFORM_HOST=""
# The folder every built app is filed into; listApps reads from it.
BASE44_APPS_FOLDER_ID=""
# The host Base44 serves your workspace's built apps from. Unlike everything
# above it is NOT a secret: preview and published app URLs are built in the
# browser to be iframed or linked, so this one is NEXT_PUBLIC_. A published app
# is `<slug>.<host>` and its preview is `preview--<slug>.<host>`. Unset, the UI
# simply shows no preview. A bare apex, e.g. `apps.example.com` — a scheme or a
# trailing slash is stripped if you leave one on.
NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE44_APP_HOST=""

# --- Inbound webhooks from Base44 ---
# There is deliberately no shared secret. /api/base44/webhooks verifies an
# Ed25519 signature, and this shell only ever holds PUBLIC keys — so nothing
# here can forge an event, and none of the three values below is sensitive
# except the registration key.
#
# The workspace's public keys, pinned. `whpk_`-prefixed, space-separated,
# printed by `npm run webhook:register`. Set it and verification makes no
# network call at all: the fetch would otherwise sit on the delivery request
# path inside Base44's 15s budget, and a receiver that cannot reach the platform
# can verify nothing while that lasts. Leave it UNSET to fetch the published key
# set instead, which picks up a key rotation on its own — pinning means applying
# a rotation yourself, inside the overlap window. Pin both keys during one.
BASE44_WEBHOOK_PUBLIC_KEYS=""
# The `b44k_` key that registers the endpoint (`outbound_webhooks:write`).
# Deploy-time only — no request path reads it. Defaults to BASE44_SVC_KEY.
BASE44_WEBHOOK_KEY=""
# Where Base44 should deliver. Defaults to NEXTAUTH_URL, so production usually
# needs nothing here; set it when the two differ, or pass `--url` to the script.
# Must be a public host: Base44 refuses localhost and private addresses, and
# never follows redirects, so a bare->www hop reads as a failed delivery.
BASE44_WEBHOOK_TARGET_URL=""
# Which events to subscribe to, space- or comma-separated. Defaults to all five
# the receiver handles; `--events` on the script overrides it. Narrowing means
# Base44 writes no outbox row for the rest, so it is less traffic rather than
# traffic filtered on arrival.
BASE44_WEBHOOK_EVENT_TYPES=""

# --- Public data API for built apps ---
# No shared secret: /api/sunny accepts only viewer tokens, which are minted per user
# per app and signed with NEXTAUTH_SECRET. Set that one and this API is configured.
